`startChangeDetection(options)` is intentionally fire-and-forget but has no `.catch()` attached. If `options.presets.apply<StoryIndexGenerator>('storyIndexGenerator')` returns `null`/`undefined` or `indexGenerator.getIndex()` rejects, the resulting unhandled promise rejection will either crash the process or emit a loud warning with no actionable context. The comment correctly explains the non-`await` intent; pair it with error handling:
```ts
startChangeDetection(options).catch((error) => {
  logger.error(`Failed to start change detection: ${error instanceof Error ? error.message : String(error)}`);
});
```

`newFiles` is an `Array` and `Array.prototype.includes` is O(n). For large projects with many untracked files this becomes a nested linear scan (once per story file, per changed file). Convert `newFiles` to a `Set` after construction so membership checks are O(1):
```ts
const newFilesSet = new Set(Array.from(changes.new).map((f) => resolve(repoRoot, f)));
// …
const value = newFilesSet.has(storyFile) ? 'status-value:new' : …
```

`queue.shift()` on an `Array` is O(n) because it shifts every remaining element left. For large module graphs (hundreds of nodes) the BFS becomes O(n²) in total. A simple two-pointer approach or swapping to a proper deque (e.g. a linked-list-based queue or using index tracking) keeps each dequeue O(1). At minimum, replace the `Array` with a head-index pattern:
```ts
let head = 0;
while (head < queue.length) {
  const current = queue[head++];
  // …
}
```
